<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  092 · S1 (decision 2) — the App Group whose container holds the Library, read by
	  `LibraryLocation.appGroupIdentifier()`. Fed by the same $(ATELIER_APP_GROUP)
	  build setting as `AtelierRefsShare.entitlements`, so the identifier this process
	  ASKS for and the identifier it is GRANTED cannot disagree.
	  The host app carries the identical key in its own (generated) Info.plist. That
	  is not redundancy: `Bundle.main` inside an app extension is the extension's own
	  bundle — an extension gets no reading of its container app's plist — so this
	  copy is the only one this process can see (092 · S4b, changelog 398).
	-->
	<key>AtelierAppGroupIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(ATELIER_APP_GROUP)</string>
	<!--
	  093 § 6 — the phone is dark only, and an app extension inherits NOTHING from its
	  host app's appearance. `ShareViewController` also sets `overrideUserInterfaceStyle`;
	  this is the half that governs the system chrome around it, and a light frame
	  around a dark confirmation card is the worst of both.
	-->
	<key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key>
	<string>Dark</string>
	<key>NSExtension</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSExtensionAttributes</key>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  092 · S4b-ii · D-b — URLs and images, nothing else.

			  This replaces `TRUEPREDICATE`, under which Atelier offered itself for
			  every share on the phone including plain text, a contact and a PDF, and
			  then had nothing to do with them. The rule is the gate: what the extension
			  refuses here it does not have to fail politely at later, which is why
			  `harvest` has exactly one "nothing capturable" path and no per-type
			  branching.

			  **`SupportsWebPage` is OFF, and turning it on is the whole of tier 2**
			  (092 · S4b). It is the key that makes Safari run
			  `NSExtensionJavaScriptPreprocessingFile` against the shared page — the only
			  way a share sheet sees a DOM, and so the only way the phone can match the
			  browser extension on the auth-walled sites (`PageResolver.isAuthWalledHost`)
			  where a cookie-less fetch from the Mac sees a login wall instead of the post.

			  It was on for a day and came back off, and the reason is worth keeping in
			  front of whoever turns it on again (421):

			    · With this key set, Safari sends the page item INSTEAD OF a URL item.
			      The share then carries exactly one attachment, `com.apple.property-list`,
			      and `attributedContentText` holds the page TITLE — there is no URL
			      anywhere.
			    · On the iOS 26.5 simulator that attachment never loads: `loadItem` and
			      `loadDataRepresentation` both fail with `NSItemProviderErrorDomain -1000`
			      over `NSCocoaErrorDomain 4101` — a dead connection to Safari's web
			      content process, i.e. the script's results were never produced.
			    · Those two facts together turn a share that used to become a LINK capture
			      into a lost one. Tier 2 not working is acceptable; tier 2 not working
			      being worse than tier 1 is not.

			  Everything behind the key is written, tested and inert: `PagePreprocessor.js`
			  ships in this bundle, `AtelierCapture.PageHarvest` / `PageExtractor` decide
			  what a snapshot means, `SharedItem.page` drafts it, and
			  `Tier2ShareUITests` drives the whole path through Safari. Turning both keys
			  on and running that test on a DEVICE is the remaining step — a simulator's
			  WebKit is the likeliest explanation for the failure above, and it is not
			  something a simulator can be asked to prove.

			  `SupportsWebURL` and `SupportsImage` are the tier-1 offers and stay: a link
			  out of Safari, Mail, Messages or a native app, and a photo.

			  Omitted keys default to zero/false, so `SupportsText`, `SupportsMovie`,
			  `SupportsFile` and the rest are off by not being here.
			-->
			<key>NSExtensionActivationRule</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSExtensionActivationSupportsWebURLWithMaxCount</key>
				<integer>1</integer>
				<key>NSExtensionActivationSupportsImageWithMaxCount</key>
				<integer>1</integer>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  093 · D-c — a principal class, not a storyboard. `Base.lproj/MainInterface.storyboard`
		  and the `SLComposeServiceViewController` it instantiated are gone: the compose
		  sheet is a form with a text field and a Post button, and 093 § 1 decides
		  post-and-dismiss with no form at all. It was also the only storyboard in a
		  repo that is SwiftUI throughout.

		  Module-qualified because the class is a plain Swift `UIViewController`
		  subclass with no `@objc` name: the runtime symbol is mangled, and
		  `$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME)` resolves it without an ObjC-visibility attribute
		  existing only to satisfy a plist.
		-->
		<key>NSExtensionPrincipalClass</key>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).ShareViewController</string>
		<key>NSExtensionPointIdentifier</key>
		<string>com.apple.share-services</string>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
